Seven bugs in liblocation:

- liblocation.so.0 named none of the libraries it uses (GLib, GObject,
  GConf, libdbus, dbus-glib): programs that did not link them themselves
  (a Qt program's plugin) failed to load it.
- dbus_g_proxy_call() takes the input arguments, G_TYPE_INVALID, then the
  output arguments and another G_TYPE_INVALID. The calls of location-ui's
  "display" and "close" ended after the inputs, so dbus-glib read whatever
  followed in the variable arguments as the first output's type. Where
  that is not 0 every dialog request failed ("Too few arguments in
  reply") although location-ui showed the dialog: on FreeBSD/arm64, and
  with Leste's own liblocation0 0.108-1+4m7 under qemu in a chroot of
  Leste's arm64 image (tests/leste-location); where it happens to be 0
  the request works.
- get_selected_method() had its test the wrong way round: a client's
  LOCATION_METHOD_USER_SELECTED (0, what most apps and liblocation's own
  example ask for) gave no method at all, a specific method every method.
  After the "enable positioning" dialog the user's yes therefore ended in
  LOCATION_ERROR_USER_REJECTED_DIALOG ("User didn't enable requested
  methods"), with GPS and network just switched on in GConf, and
  location_gpsd_control_get_allowed_methods() always returned 0. Now 0
  stands for every method the user allows (cell positioning only with the
  network allowed, so that the dialog answered with nothing still fails),
  a specific one for itself, as the code below the test already treats
  them.

- The last of LocationGPSDevice's match rules,
  "type='signal',interface='com.nokia.Location.Uncertainty", lacked its
  closing quote: the bus answered every device's AddMatch (and its
  RemoveMatch) with MatchRuleInvalid ("Unbalanced quotation marks in
  match rule"). The rule is for Nokia's liblas, which nothing on Leste
  runs, and the signal filter has no handler for that interface: the
  accuracy comes from location-daemon's org.maemo.LocationDaemon.Accuracy,
  whose rule is right, so no data was lost, only an error per device.
- Creating a LocationGPSDControl (location_gpsd_control_get_default())
  started positioning: its init reads GConf's method pair (which
  location-control's schema always sets) through get_location_method(),
  which then called location_gpsd_control_prestart_internal(TRUE), so the
  privacy disclaimer or the "enable positioning" dialog opened, or
  location-daemon was started (and with it gpsd), before the app called
  location_gpsd_control_start(), or when it never did (MeeCast only
  watches the gpsd_running and gpsd_stopped signals; GPS Recorder and
  rtcom-presence-ui set preferred-method and -interval after creating the
  control, so the early start ignored them). Maemo 5's API says that
  location_gpsd_control_start() is how "the application informs that it
  wants to use the location service", and location-control's schema that
  the start shows the enable dialog. Leste's reimplementation had it right
  at first (19328e5: restarted after a method change only if it had been
  running, "stopped"); 6dcbd87 (the port to gpsd) put "!p->gpsd_running"
  in that place, true wherever nothing runs. Now it restarts, and reports
  gpsd_stopped, only where positioning was running.

- gpsd_shutdown() no longer cleared gpsd_running (since Leste's fb6729a,
  which started location-daemon over D-Bus). Once positioning had run,
  start_internal() returned at once: location_gpsd_control_start() after
  location_gpsd_control_stop() did nothing (no gpsd_running signal, no
  start of location-daemon), and the restart after a change of the
  method, the settings or MCE's device mode was none.
- Every LocationGPSDControl took the shared lock on
  /run/lock/location-daemon.lock in its init and kept it until it was
  freed. location-daemon runs as long as any client holds that lock (it
  tries an exclusive one every 15 s and exits when it gets it), so a
  control that was created and never started, or stopped, kept the
  daemon, and gpsd, running. The lock is now held only while positioning
  runs: taken before location-daemon's start is called, dropped when
  positioning ends (stop(), the control freed). Maemo 5's API: "Location
  service is kept running as long as there is at least one application
  using it". As the daemon removes the file when it exits, the lock is
  taken again when it was on a file just removed.

The same fixes are in FreeBSD's Hildon build
(freebsd/hildon/patches/liblocation/0002, 0005 to 0010).

